  "query_preview": "..\n\nQuiet weather expected through the weekend as high pressure settles \nover the region. Upper level long wave flow will be low amplitude \nand progressive with the bulk of the flow locked over southern \nCanada as the southern conus ridge continues to hold. Main surface \nbaroclinic zone remains draped along the Ohio Valley north of the \nridge which will keep the bulk of the precip south of the region. \nincoming mid level shortwave will be positively tilted which will \ndelay forcing the remnant thermal trough out of the region so expect \na slowly modifying airmass today through Saturday. Once the mid \nlevel trough axis passes east Saturday, warmer southerly flow will \nreturn to the region providing a return to the lower 80s briefly \nahead of a cold front. \n\nThe front will sweep through the region late Saturday evening and \nlooks to be free of precip as the model sounding show a great deal \nof dry air through the column to contend with and the forcing will \nbe well to the north over Ontario. The one thing to watch for will \nbe a plume of moisture overriding the boundary to the south Saturday \nwhich will try to reach into far southern MI. Most likely, this will \nbe held to the south as the front compresses the ridging and pushes \nit east making that region unfavorable to northward advancements of \nthe moisture plume, but a slower front may allow some moisture to \nreach a little farther north. Next chance of showers and \nthunderstorms arrives Sunday night as a more amplified trough passes \nover the region.",
